// ---------------------------------------------------------------- passages (TFNG / YNNG)
const P_BEES = "Over the past decade, rooftop beekeeping has spread across several European capitals. Supporters claim that city bees often produce more honey than country bees, because parks and gardens offer a wider variety of flowers throughout the year. However, a recent study of 40 hives in one city found that some colonies struggled to find enough food in late summer, when many flowers had finished blooming. Those colonies made less honey and went into winter with smaller stores, which made them more likely to die before spring. The researchers did not suggest banning rooftop hives. Instead, they recommended that city councils plant more late-flowering species, such as ivy, and that new beekeepers be limited to a small number of hives per street. They added that their figures came from a single city and should not be applied elsewhere without further checks.";
const P_FOURDAY = "Several companies have trialled a four-day working week, and the early results are encouraging: staff reported lower stress, and many managers noticed no fall in output. Yet I remain cautious about presenting this as a solution for every workplace. Hospitals, shops and schools must be staffed on five or even seven days, so a shorter week could simply mean the same people working longer shifts. Supporters point out that a shorter week costs employers little when output is measured by results rather than by hours at a desk. I accept that argument for office jobs, but it says little about work that cannot be moved to another day. It is also unwise to rely on results from firms that volunteered to take part, since they were probably the ones most likely to succeed. More evidence from a wider range of employers is needed before governments consider making the change.";
const P_TEEN = "Research on adolescent sleep suggests that teenagers' body clocks shift later during puberty, making it natural for them to feel alert late at night and sleepy in the early morning. Several schools that moved their start time from 8.00 to 9.00 reported fewer late arrivals and a small improvement in attendance. Test results, however, showed no clear change in the first year. Sleep specialists add that teenagers who regularly sleep for fewer than eight hours are more likely to report low mood and difficulty concentrating in class. Some parents worried that later finishes would interfere with sport and part-time jobs, but few schools received serious complaints once the new timetable had settled. All of the schools in the trial were in towns, so it is not yet known whether rural schools, where many pupils travel long distances by bus, would see the same effects.";
const P_PLASTIC = "Scientists sampling twelve rivers across the region found tiny pieces of plastic in every sample. The highest concentrations were downstream of large towns, but even a river in a protected mountain area contained traces. Most of the particles came from synthetic clothing fibres washed out in domestic laundry, rather than from larger items of litter breaking down. Concentrations also rose after heavy rain, probably because overflowing sewage systems carried more fibres into the water. Water companies currently filter out about 90 per cent of such particles, although no regulation requires them to do so. The researchers recommended that filters should be fitted to new washing machines, a measure that several manufacturers are now testing, and they argued that clearer labelling of clothes made from synthetic materials would help consumers to make better choices.";
const P_BILING = "Children who grow up speaking two languages often take slightly longer to produce their first words in each language, but their total vocabulary across both languages is usually equal to or larger than that of children who speak one language. Some parents fear that a second language will confuse a child, yet studies suggest that young bilinguals separate the two systems from a very early age. The two languages of a bilingual child are rarely used in the same situations, so a child may know the word for a kitchen object at home but not at school. For this reason, tests that count words in only one language can make bilingual children look weaker than they really are. The advantage most consistently reported is not in intelligence but in attention control: bilingual children tend to switch between tasks more easily.";
const P_DESAL = "Desalination plants turn seawater into drinking water and supply much of the water in some Gulf states. The process once used so much energy that it was considered a last resort. Modern membrane technology has cut energy use by roughly half since the 1990s, and some new plants are partly powered by solar panels. In certain countries more than half of the drinking water now comes from desalination, and the proportion is expected to grow as populations rise. Plants are usually built beside power stations so that waste heat can be used in the process. Nevertheless, the plants produce a concentrated salt solution that is usually returned to the sea, and the effect of this on marine life near the outlets is still being studied. Engineers are now looking for ways to reuse the salt, for example in the chemical industry.";
const P_MUSEUM = "When the city museum abolished its entrance fee in 2019, visitor numbers rose by 60 per cent in the first year. However, the increase came mainly from people who already lived within walking distance; the proportion of visitors from the poorest neighbourhoods rose only from 7 to 9 per cent. Staff concluded that cost was only one of the obstacles, and that lack of information and a feeling that museums are 'not for people like me' also kept some residents away. A survey of visitors showed that one in three had come for the first time, and that most of them had heard about the museum from friends rather than from advertising. The museum has since begun running events in community centres, and the council has agreed to continue funding the scheme for a further three years.";
const P_CORAL = "Coral bleaching occurs when warm water causes corals to expel the algae that live in their tissue and give them colour and food. A bleached coral is not dead, and it can recover if temperatures fall within a few weeks. Repeated bleaching, however, leaves corals weaker, and a reef that bleaches every two or three years may never fully regrow. Bleaching has become more frequent as ocean temperatures rise, and even a coral that recovers tends to grow more slowly and to produce fewer young for some time afterwards. Some species appear to tolerate heat better than others, which has led researchers to experiment with moving them to reefs that are more at risk. Local conditions also matter: reefs that are not damaged by pollution or overfishing seem to bounce back more quickly than those that are.";
const P_CYCLE = "Building cycle lanes is often presented as a simple way to reduce traffic. I doubt it is that simple. In cities where lanes were added without any restriction on car parking, the lanes were frequently blocked by delivery vehicles, and cyclists soon returned to the road. In one city I know, the council spent heavily on a network of lanes, yet the number of cyclists hardly changed in five years, because many of the routes ended suddenly at busy junctions. By contrast, the few cities that combined lanes with higher parking charges saw a measurable fall in short car journeys. Critics will say that parking charges are unpopular with voters, and that is true, but politicians who avoid them should not claim to be serious about traffic. It seems clear to me that cycle lanes work best as part of a wider set of measures rather than on their own.";
const P_APPS = "Language-learning apps have attracted millions of users, and their short daily lessons suit busy lives. A survey of 2,000 users found that two thirds had kept using their app for more than three months, but only one in ten said they could hold a basic conversation in the language. Teachers point out that apps are good at vocabulary and recognition but give little practice in speaking spontaneously. Researchers who followed a smaller group for a year found that daily practice fell steeply after the first few weeks, especially among users who had set no clear goal. Those who combined the app with a weekly class progressed fastest. Several app companies have responded by adding live tutoring, which is usually offered at an extra cost.";
const P_TREES = "Planting trees along city streets lowers summer temperatures, absorbs some air pollution and can raise property values. Yet the benefits depend heavily on which species are chosen and where they are planted. Large trees with dense canopies give the most shade, but they need space for roots, and roots that have been squeezed by pavements can lift the surface and damage pipes. Maintenance is another consideration. Street trees must be pruned, and fallen leaves can block drains in autumn, so cities with limited budgets have to weigh these costs against the benefits. For that reason, some councils now plant smaller species in narrow streets and keep larger ones for parks and wide avenues. A few have also asked residents to water young trees during dry summers.";
const P_SHOPS = "Online shopping has grown quickly, yet physical shops have not disappeared as many predicted. Sales data from one country show that online purchases made up 28 per cent of retail spending in 2022, compared with 19 per cent in 2016. Over the same period the number of shop premises fell by about 7 per cent. Analysts note that many online orders are collected in stores, and that customers who visit a shop to try a product often spend more than those who buy only online. One chain of electrical retailers has responded by turning part of each store into a showroom and collection point, and its profits have recovered. Online-only companies have noticed the same effect, and several have begun to open small shops of their own.";
const P_STAR = "The first telescope images of the star's surroundings suggested a ring of dust, but astronomers were cautious. Dust rings are common around young stars, and this star is estimated to be at least three billion years old, much older than usual. A second team, using a different instrument, detected the same ring and measured its temperature. Their results are consistent with the dust being left over from a collision between two small planets, although the team admit that other explanations cannot yet be excluded. If the collision explanation is correct, the ring should contain fragments ranging from fine dust to lumps of rock. Further observations are planned for next year, when a more powerful telescope is due to begin operating, and the team hope that these will show how the ring is shaped.";
const P_REMOTE = "Since more employees began working from home, some city-centre cafes and sandwich bars have lost up to a third of their weekday customers. At the same time, shops and restaurants in suburbs have reported higher sales. Property experts expect office rents in city centres to stay low for several years, although they disagree about whether the trend will continue if companies ask staff to return for three days a week. Office landlords are already responding: some are offering shorter leases, and others have begun to convert floors into flexible spaces that companies can hire by the day. Some local governments are considering turning empty offices into housing, and one city has offered a grant to owners who agree to make the change.";
const P_ANTIB = "Antibiotics lose their effect when bacteria evolve resistance, and the more often a drug is used, the faster this happens. Resistant infections are harder to treat and often lead to longer stays in hospital, so reducing unnecessary prescriptions benefits patients as well as health budgets. Doctors in some countries still prescribe antibiotics for viral infections such as colds, on which they have no effect. Patients themselves sometimes ask for the drugs, and some doctors admit that they give in to avoid an argument. In a trial in one region, doctors who received a monthly letter comparing their prescribing with that of their colleagues cut unnecessary prescriptions by 12 per cent. The letters cost almost nothing to produce, which has encouraged health officials to try them elsewhere.";
const P_ROADS = "Historians once believed that the Roman road network was built mainly for military use. More recent research points to a wider purpose. Along many routes, archaeologists have found remains of inns, markets and workshops that appear to date from the first century, suggesting regular civilian traffic. Milestones beside some roads also record repairs that were paid for by local towns rather than by the army, which implies that nearby communities depended on the roads for trade. Letters preserved on wooden tablets mention merchants complaining about delays on the road, although none of these refer to the main military routes in the north. Taken together, the evidence suggests that the network grew in stages, with civilian needs shaping its development as much as the needs of the legions.";
const P_STORE = "Solar and wind power produce electricity only when the sun shines or the wind blows, so storing surplus energy is a central challenge. Lithium batteries are widely used for short-term storage, usually for a few hours. For longer periods, other methods are needed: one is to use surplus electricity to pump water uphill, then release it through turbines when demand rises. Pumped storage is already common, but it requires suitable geography, and few countries have many sites left. Another option is to use surplus electricity to split water into hydrogen, which can be kept in tanks and burned or used in fuel cells later, although about half of the energy is lost in the process. Several governments have started to fund research into both approaches.";
const P_READING = "It is often claimed that young people no longer read. The evidence is more complicated. In my own survey of 300 undergraduates, nearly all said they read daily, but most of that reading was messages, social media posts and short articles. Only a fifth had finished a book in the previous month. Several of the students told me that they found it hard to stay focused on a long text for more than twenty minutes. I do not think this makes them less intelligent than earlier generations, but I would argue that sustained, long-form reading is a skill that needs regular practice, and schools should make more time for it. Some colleagues disagree, believing that short digital reading is a new form of literacy that deserves equal respect. I understand that view, although I would not want it to replace the habit of reading whole books.";
const P_ISLAND = "Visitor numbers to the island of Marlow rose from 20,000 to 150,000 a year in a decade, bringing new jobs but also pressure on water supplies. Residents use less water per head than tourists, but because tourists are so numerous, they account for roughly two thirds of total consumption in summer. During the driest months, water is already brought in by tanker, which residents say is both costly and unreliable. The island government has introduced a tourist tax, with the income to be spent on a new water-recycling plant. Hotels that have their own wells have been asked to record how much they pump. Many residents support the tax, although some argue that it should be higher. Hotel owners have objected to it, saying that it will drive visitors to neighbouring islands.";
const P_NOTES = "A well-known experiment compared students who typed notes on laptops with those who wrote by hand. Laptop users wrote more words but tended to copy lectures almost word for word, while handwriters, who could not keep up, summarised ideas in their own words. When tested a week later, the handwriters answered conceptual questions better, although the two groups scored similarly on factual questions. The researchers suggested that the effort of summarising forces handwriters to process the material more deeply. Some teachers now ask students to put laptops away during lectures, while others teach them how to summarise on screen. The study has been widely quoted in the press, although its authors have warned against drawing firm conclusions from a single experiment. Critics point out that the sample was small and consisted entirely of students from one university.";
const P_CORRIDOR = "Roads and farmland split the habitat of many large mammals into isolated patches. Where populations cannot move between patches, they become inbred and more vulnerable to disease. Wildlife corridors, strips of natural vegetation connecting the patches, are one remedy. A ten-year project linking two forest reserves found that leopards began using the corridor within two years, although smaller animals took much longer. Corridors are not a perfect solution: they must be wide enough to protect animals from hunters and traffic, and in crowded regions it can be difficult to buy the land. Even so, conservationists regard them as a more practical option than moving animals by lorry from one reserve to another. The cost of the project was about the same as building four kilometres of motorway.";
const P_VOC = "In some European countries, up to half of secondary-school pupils follow vocational courses that combine classroom study with paid work placements, while in others the figure is below ten per cent. Employers in the first group say that graduates arrive with practical skills, and youth unemployment in those countries tends to be lower. However, economists warn against assuming that apprenticeships are the cause; countries with strong apprenticeship systems often also have strong economies for other reasons. Some countries with small vocational sectors have begun to expand them in the hope of reproducing the results. Critics, however, warn that students who specialise early may find it harder to change career later, and they argue that general education should remain the main route for most young people.";

